course: english
lesson: 12
title: Prefixes: un-, dis-, mis-, re-, pre-, in-, im-, il-, ir-
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A prefix is a few letters added to the front of a word that change its meaning: happy, unhappy; do, redo; view, preview. The best news for spellers: a prefix never changes the spelling of the root word. Write the prefix, then the whole word, letter for letter.
explain: That one rule explains the famous double letters. dis + appear = disappear (one s, because «appear» has none), but dis + satisfied = dissatisfied (two s's). mis + spell = misspell, with both s's. il + legal = illegal, ir + regular = irregular, im + mature = immature. The doubles are not mistakes; they are the end of the prefix meeting the start of the word.
explain: Several prefixes mean «not», and the first letter of the root often picks which: im- before m or p (impossible, immature), il- before l (illegal, illogical), ir- before r (irregular, irresponsible), in- for many others (incorrect, invisible), and un- for very many common words (unhappy, unkind). So «unpossible» is wrong; it is «impossible».
explain: Others carry their own meaning: re- again (redo, rewrite), mis- wrongly (misread, misspell), dis- not or the opposite (disagree, disrespect), pre- before (preview, prehistoric), sub- under (submarine, subheading), auto- self (autobiography, automatic), bi- two (bicycle), tri- three (tricycle). Un- can also undo an action: tie, untie; lock, unlock; zip, unzip.
explain: Prefixes let you guess new words. Aryan in Delhi says the teacher will «prepone» the test: pre- (before) is the opposite of post- (after), so prepone means bring forward. It is correct Indian English, though rare in Britain, where people say «bring forward».
fact: A prefix is added to the start of a word and changes its meaning.
fact: Adding a prefix never changes the spelling of the root word: dis + appear = disappear; mis + spell = misspell.
fact: Prefixes meaning «not» include un-, in-, im-, il- and ir-.
fact: im- is used before many words starting with m or p: impossible, immature, impatient.
fact: il- is used before l (illegal, illogical) and ir- before r (irregular, irresponsible), so these words have double letters.
fact: re- means again: redo, rewrite, replay.
fact: mis- means wrongly: misread, misspell, misbehave.
fact: dis- means not or the opposite: disagree, dislike, disrespect; the slang «diss» comes from «disrespect».
fact: pre- means before (preview, prehistoric); sub- means under (submarine, subway).
fact: auto- means self (autobiography, automatic); bi- means two (bicycle); tri- means three (tricycle).
fact: un- can mean «not» (unhappy) or undo an action (untie, unlock).
fact: «Prepone» means bring forward in Indian English; in Britain people say «bring forward».
fact: Slang in this lesson: «fixin' to» means «about to» in the US South; «love» is a friendly way to address someone in much of Britain.
exercise: Ella writes «unpossible». What is right? | impossible | inpossible | umpossible | dispossible
exercise: Jamie writes «dissappear». Why is that wrong? | dis + appear: «appear» has no s, so only one s | The prefix is «diss-» with two s's | It should be «disapear», with one p | Nothing; both spellings are fine
exercise: Why does «illegal» have two l's? | il- plus «legal», which starts with l | The l is doubled to keep it short | It's an exception to learn by heart | Because legal ends in l
exercise: Ben in Houston is «fixin' to» redo his homework. What does «re-» mean? | Again | Wrongly | Before | Not
exercise: Grace writes «mispell». What is right? | misspell | misspel | mispel | missppell
exercise: What does «mis-» mean? | Wrongly | Again, once more | Under, below | Self, on your own
exercise: Zara's gran says «disrespectful». What does «dis-» do here? | Makes the opposite of respectful | Makes the word mean very, very respectful | Makes it mean before | Makes it mean again
exercise: Theo asks if «pre-» means before. Is it so? | Yes: preview, prehistoric | No, it means after something | No, it means under or below | Yes, but only in Indian English
exercise: What does Aryan's teacher mean by «prepone the test»? | Move it earlier | Cancel it for good | Move it to later on | Make it harder for everyone
exercise: Jess asks if «irregular» is ir + regular. Is it? | Yes, so it has two r's | No, it's i + rregular | No, it's irr + egular, a different root | Yes, so it has one r
exercise: Nathan learns «auto-». What does it mean? | Self | Car | Fast | Two
exercise: Ruby unties her shoe. What does «un-» mean here? | Undo the action | Do it a second time | Do it the wrong way | Do it before anyone else does
exercise: What does «sub-» mean in «submarine»? | Under | Over | Again | Self
exercise: How many wheels does a tricycle have, and why? | Three, because tri- means three | Two, because tri- means try | Four, because tri- means twice two | One, because tri- means only
exercise: Which word is spelled correctly? | dissatisfied | disatisfied | dissattisfied | dissatisfyed
exercise: What does a prefix do to the spelling of the root? | Nothing, the root stays the same | It drops the first letter of the root | It doubles the last letter of the root | It changes the root's vowel
test: «I'm fixin' to misdo my homework cos I messed up». What should he say, and why? | «Redo», because re- means again | «Misdo», because mis- means again | «Undo», because un- means again | «Predo», because pre- means again
test: «dis + appear, so double s, dissappear, sorted». Where's the slip? | «Appear» has no s, so it's «disappear» | Nothing, «dissappear» is right | It should be «disapear» | It should be «dissapear», two s's
test: «unlegal, cos un- means not innit». What is the real word? | illegal | inlegal | imlegal | dislegal
test: What would happen if we wrote «misspell» with one s? | We'd misspell «misspell», because both s's belong | Nothing, «mispell» is also correct | It would mean «spell again» | It would mean «not spell»
test: «my cousin in Delhi said they'll prepone the test, he's made that up». Is he? | No, it is correct Indian English for «bring forward» | Yes, «prepone» is not a word anywhere | Yes, he means «postpone» | No, it means «cancel», like «call off»
test: «dissed me in the playground, so dis- is just slang». What's true? | Dis- is a real prefix; the slang «diss» comes from «disrespect» | Dis- is only slang and never used in real words | Dis- means again, like re- | Dis- came from text messages, so it isn't real
test: A cartoon robot says «I am unpossible to beat!». What should it say? | impossible | inpossible | unpossable | dispossible
test: «bicycle's got two wheels, so a tricycle must have... four?» What is right? | Three, because tri- means three | Four, because tri- means twice two | Two, like a bicycle | One, because tri- means only one
test: Gran: «don't be disrespectful, love». What is the root word, and what does the prefix do? | respectful; dis- makes the opposite | respect; dis- means again | spectful; disre- means not | disrespect; -ful means not
test: ★★★ Which word has a double letter ONLY because a prefix meets the root? | unnatural | rabbit | little | slippery
test: ★★★ «Responsible», «logical», «mature», «correct». Which list gives their «not» words correctly? | irresponsible, illogical, immature, incorrect | unresponsible, unlogical, unmature, uncorrect | inresponsible, inlogical, inmature, incorrect | irresponsible, illogical, inmature, uncorrect
test: ★★★ «Subheading» means a heading under the main one. What would «sub-zero» most likely mean? | Below zero | A little above zero | Exactly zero | Zero, again and again
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the effect of a prefix upon the spelling of a root word. | The spelling of the root word remains unaltered | The first letter of the root word is invariably omitted | The final letter of the prefix is invariably doubled | The spelling of the root word is determined anew in each case
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the word «illegal» contains a double l. | The prefix il- precedes a root word commencing with the letter l | The letter l is doubled in all words of legal significance | The double l is an irregularity for which no explanation has been advanced by grammarians | The word was borrowed from French with its double l unchanged
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the prefix re-? | It signifies repetition, as in «redo» and «rewrite» | It signifies error, as in «misread» and «misspell» | It signifies negation, as in «unhappy» and «unkind» | It signifies anteriority, as in «preview»
exam: It is given that the word «prepone» is employed by a teacher in Delhi. Ascertain its meaning. | To bring forward to an earlier time | To defer to a later time | To cancel without notice | To repeat the examination upon a subsequent occasion
